WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.360 --> 00:00:08.700
This is my morning commute.

00:00:09.480 --> 00:00:15.600
I get up, get ready, get in a car, drive to the train station,

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:19.520
look for parking, get on a train for an hour and a half,

00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:25.440
arrive at Penn Station, get another train,
walk five minutes, and start my work day.

00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:27.260
Is this killing me?

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:39.860
Before you go and post that comment
telling me to move closer, you need to

00:00:39.860 --> 00:00:44.840
see that I'm not the only person doing
this. This is the train station I commute

00:00:44.840 --> 00:00:49.490
from. This place is huge and yet it's
full almost every single day. Just this

00:00:49.490 --> 00:00:52.850
past fall they had to rip out an entire
section of trees to make more space for

00:00:52.850 --> 00:00:55.260
parking and it's still not always enough.

00:00:57.080 --> 00:00:59.000
According to US census data the average

00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:02.480
American commute is nearly 30 minutes
one way, a number that's been on the rise

00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:06.410
for the last few decades. In general,
people are choosing to commute further

00:01:06.410 --> 00:01:10.729
and farther than ever before. And those
long grueling hours in cars, on trains,

00:01:10.729 --> 00:01:15.020
or buses can take a toll on a person's
health. Especially when we all hate it so

00:01:15.020 --> 00:01:16.980
very, very, much.

00:01:18.680 --> 00:01:21.220
If you commute or you've been on the

00:01:21.220 --> 00:01:25.560
internet at all, you've seen the articles
and maybe you've read the studies.

00:01:25.560 --> 00:01:28.400
Commuting has been linked to higher
rates of obesity, stress, anxiety,

00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:32.660
depression, higher blood pressure, higher
rates of divorce, neck and back problems,

00:01:32.660 --> 00:01:35.440
shorter lifespans, and you get the point.

00:01:35.900 --> 00:01:38.420
Commuting long distances just isn't good

00:01:38.420 --> 00:01:42.200
for you. But a lot of it comes down to
how you do it. For example a Canadian

00:01:42.410 --> 00:01:46.160
study of commuters in Montreal found
that people who walk, bike, or hop on a

00:01:46.160 --> 00:01:50.149
train had a higher life satisfaction than
those with comparable commute times on

00:01:50.149 --> 00:01:53.750
other types of transit. In another study,
British researchers found that people

00:01:53.750 --> 00:01:57.800
who drive to work instead of using
public transportation are more likely to

00:01:57.800 --> 00:02:02.330
be obese. Sitting in a car all day really
adds the negative effects of an inactive

00:02:02.330 --> 00:02:06.289
lifestyle. You're sitting for hours on
end and when all is said and done it's

00:02:06.289 --> 00:02:11.360
really hard to find the time or the
motivation to exercise. And that's not all.

00:02:11.360 --> 00:02:15.720
Research has shown that driving alone in
particular can make you more miserable.

00:02:15.720 --> 00:02:17.140
You're alone with your thoughts for
hours,

00:02:17.150 --> 00:02:21.650
often in frustrating situations, so it's
not really surprising that this is the

00:02:21.650 --> 00:02:26.060
disaster for both your mental and
physical health. Yet according to a 2010

00:02:26.060 --> 00:02:31.010
report, this is exactly how 80% of
Americans commute to work every single day.

00:02:31.940 --> 00:02:33.640
Now by comparison, those who take public

00:02:33.650 --> 00:02:37.550
transportation are slightly healthier
and slightly more satisfied with life.

00:02:37.550 --> 00:02:41.200
Yet we tend to be more negative about
our experience overall.

00:02:44.560 --> 00:02:45.280
Public transit

00:02:45.290 --> 00:02:49.340
commuters often resent the timetables. If
I don't leave work at 6 o'clock on the

00:02:49.340 --> 00:02:52.580
dot, I won't catch the 6:21. if I don't catch

00:02:52.580 --> 00:02:56.450
the 6:21 I have to get on the 6:53, and
that's the difference between me getting

00:02:56.450 --> 00:03:01.940
home at 8:00 p.m. and me getting home at
8:45. Throw in things like transfers,

00:03:01.940 --> 00:03:05.780
delays, cancellations, and you have a
really solid foundation for a very

00:03:05.780 --> 00:03:08.100
stressful, very annoying commute.

00:03:10.640 --> 00:03:13.580
Basically none of this is looking good
for my mortality.

00:03:16.020 --> 00:03:17.420
Then why do so many of

00:03:17.420 --> 00:03:23.690
us do it? Commuting has been viewed as a
source of stress, but in fact it gives

00:03:23.690 --> 00:03:27.680
people a break. It's sometimes the only
hour of the day that individuals have

00:03:27.680 --> 00:03:32.330
alone, to be by themselves to think, to
listen to music. Meaning my commute isn't

00:03:32.330 --> 00:03:37.040
technically dead space in my day. I catch
up on reading, I listen to podcasts, I take

00:03:37.040 --> 00:03:40.790
advantage of the alone time, but I can
also get work done when I need to. The

00:03:40.790 --> 00:03:46.010
change in communications has made
commuting an extension of the workday or

00:03:46.010 --> 00:03:49.790
an opportunity for the individual to do
things they couldn't otherwise do. It's

00:03:49.790 --> 00:03:54.500
not just a way to go from point A to
point B. In other cases ,where people

00:03:54.500 --> 00:03:58.640
live in the gig economy, having a
home that is accessible to

00:03:58.640 --> 00:04:02.630
transportation is far more important
than having a home which is next to your

00:04:02.630 --> 00:04:07.320
workplace. If you're not gonna have a
lifetime job let's have a lifetime home.

00:04:07.320 --> 00:04:10.640
So is my commute actually killing me?

00:04:10.640 --> 00:04:14.270
Well it's certainly not the best way to

00:04:14.270 --> 00:04:18.420
spend so much of my time and it is
exhausting, but you can make the most of it.

00:04:18.780 --> 00:04:22.060
And there's something to be said
about coming home at the end of it all

